/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A node is a point where two or more elements meet.
   - A branch is a single two-terminal element, a source or a passive element, between two nodes; it carries one current.
   - A loop is any closed path: start at a node, follow branches and come back to the same node without using a branch twice.
   - Meshes exist only for a planar circuit, one that can be redrawn on paper with no branch crossing another.
   - m is the number of meshes, b the number of branches and n the number of nodes.
   - Why: choose a tree, a set of n − 1 branches that touches every node and closes no loop.
   - KCL at all n nodes gives n equations, but they are not independent: add them all and every branch current appears once entering and once leaving, so the sum is 0 = 0.
   - Open and short circuits change the counts, so redraw first.
*/
