/* book Worked example: Q: For the bridge circuit in the figure (36 V source; resistors 20 Ω from A to B, 20 Ω from A to C, 10 Ω from B to C, 2 Ω from B to D, 8 Ω from C to D) find: (a) the nodes, junctions and branches; (b) the branches in a tree and the number of chords; (c) the meshes; (d) how many loops there are in all; (e) the number of independent KCL and KVL equations, with a check.
   1. (a) Nodes: A, B, C, D, so n = 4. Each node meets three elements (A: source, 20 Ω, 20 Ω; B: 20 Ω, 10 Ω, 2 Ω; C: 20 Ω, 10 Ω, 8 Ω; D: 2 Ω, 8 Ω, source), so all four are junctions. Branches: five resistors and the source, b = 6, numbered 1 to 6 in the figure.
   2. (b) A tree has n − 1 = 3 branches, for example 4, 5 and 6 (each joins one node to D). Chords: b − n + 1 = 6 − 4 + 1 = 3, here branches 1, 2 and 3.
   3. (c) m = b − n + 1 = 3. The window panes are M1 = {6, 1, 4} (source, 20 Ω, 2 Ω), M2 = {1, 2, 3} (triangle A-B-C) and M3 = {3, 4, 5} (triangle B-D-C).
   4. (d) Every set of nodes taken round a closed path gives a loop: four triangles (ABC, BCD, ABD, ACD) and three four-node paths (A-B-D-C-A, A-B-C-D-A, A-C-B-D-A), so 7 loops. Only 3 of them are independent; any other loop's KVL is a sum of mesh equations. The outer boundary {6, 2, 5} is a loop but not a mesh: branches 1, 3 and 4 lie inside it.
   5. (e) KCL equations: n − 1 = 3 (at A, B and C, with D as reference). KVL equations: m = 3. Check: 3 + 3 = 6 = b.
   Answer: n = 4, all junctions; b = 6; tree 3 branches, 3 chords; 3 meshes; 7 loops in all; 3 KCL + 3 KVL = 6 = b independent equations. */
